                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/2751: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wallace</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-04-01T16:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 1, 1920—4
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 11:10 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>879. Your 329, March 31 [<hi rendition="#italic">30</hi>], 5 p.m. to London
                    repeated to this Embassy as Department’s 641. Foreign Office was to-day informed
                    of Lamont’s proposal for an exchange of notes as outlined, of your approval and
                    of your request for the cooperation of the French Government. I am promised an
                    early expression of views which, it was indicated, would be in accord with those
                    of the British and American Governments. In fact I was informed that the Foreign
                    Office, having learned from the French group of Lamont’s proposal, has already
                    telegraphed to the French Ambassador at Tokyo approving in principle.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wallace</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
